WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620
One DNA letter is wrong among billions.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.540
How can a cell repair the right spot without rewriting good DNA?

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.160
Watch these two blue rows.

4
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.840
They carry paired information.

5
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.760
A red mark changes one block on the upper row, while the lower row stays intact.

6
00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.400
The problem's shape determines the first move.

7
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:30.893
Repair outcomes depend on what is damaged and what intact information remains.

8
00:00:32.273 --> 00:00:37.353
Sometimes the original base is still recognizable beneath an added chemical group.

9
00:00:38.753 --> 00:00:41.193
Direct reversal removes that change in place.

10
00:00:42.353 --> 00:00:46.713
The backbone stays uncut, and no partner strand serves as a template.

11
00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:52.653
This route fits only damage with chemistry an enzyme can directly undo.

12
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.470
A small damaged base needs a different move.

13
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.210
Base excision repair removes one block from the upper row.

14
00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:06.790
The lower row guides a replacement into that position, and the strand is sealed.

15
00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:11.230
One damaged block becomes one restored green block.

16
00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.670
Most surrounding DNA remains untouched.

17
00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.750
Repair machinery works locally, preserving the surrounding sequence.

18
00:01:20.883 --> 00:01:25.063
Ultraviolet damage can join neighboring bases and bend the row.

19
00:01:26.423 --> 00:01:30.103
Nucleotide excision repair responds to the distorted stretch.

20
00:01:31.303 --> 00:01:34.263
Two cuts lift out a short patch instead of one base.

21
00:01:35.543 --> 00:01:38.783
The intact row guides several replacements across the gap.

22
00:01:40.003 --> 00:01:42.223
The same partner supplies information.

23
00:01:43.723 --> 00:01:48.203
Lesion chemistry and distortion choose the pathway, which sets how much is removed.

24
00:01:48.203 --> 00:01:49.203
Nucleotide excision repair responds to the distorted stretch.

25
00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:52.807
Now both letters are ordinary, yet they pair incorrectly.

26
00:01:54.307 --> 00:01:55.307
Neither looks damaged.

27
00:01:56.547 --> 00:02:00.187
If the strands carry no history, which base should repair replace?

28
00:02:01.607 --> 00:02:03.627
Choose the upper block or the lower block.

29
00:02:09.533 --> 00:02:11.553
The mismatch alone cannot answer.

30
00:02:13.053 --> 00:02:16.393
Repair needs another clue identifying the newly made strand.

31
00:02:17.813 --> 00:02:21.193
During replication, the template existed before the error.

32
00:02:22.613 --> 00:02:26.753
Signals that tell new DNA from old direct removal past the mismatch.

33
00:02:27.973 --> 00:02:30.473
The template then guides a corrected patch.

34
00:02:31.713 --> 00:02:36.453
Here, trustworthy information includes strand identity, not just the partner's

35
00:02:36.453 --> 00:02:40.373
letters. That extra label solves the ambiguity.

36
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.760
Carry the same picture through the choices.

37
00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.800
A reversibly damaged base is restored in place.

38
00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.900
A small lesion loses one base.

39
00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.060
A bulky distortion loses a patch.

40
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.780
A mismatch loses part of the strand identified as new.

41
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.000
Each route limits its work to the problem it recognizes.

42
00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.120
The excision routes copy the intact partner to restore the strand.

43
00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.160
If repair misses a lesion, replication may turn damage into a stable sequence

44
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.380
change. Repair can also change sequence when information is missing.

45
00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.640
That is why finding damage is only half the problem.

46
00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.340
The cell must also know what information remains trustworthy.

47
00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.600
Follow that question whenever you choose a repair route.
